/**
 * hello.tks — the reference source of the debugger proof-of-concept.
 *
 * Deliberately two frames deep: `main` calls `add`, the breakpoint lands inside `add`, so a
 * `bt` has TWO frames to print and a wrong unwind is visible instead of merely suspected.
 * Nothing else lives here — every extra line is a line the hand-written DWARF in `mini.s`
 * would have to describe.
 *
 * THIS FILE IS NEVER COMPILED. It is the source the hand-written DWARF in `mini.s` POINTS
 * AT, and that isolation is the whole point of the proof: the debugger lists this Teko text
 * while single-stepping machine code that no Teko compiler produced. What is being proved is
 * the DWARF, not the compiler.
 *
 * @see mini.s        the hand-written object whose DWARF names this file
 * @see reproduce.sh  the end-to-end command that runs gdb and lldb against it
 * @since 0.3.1 debugger proof-of-concept
 */

/**
 * add — sum two integers, the CALLEE frame of the proof.
 *
 * The breakpoint of the proof is set on the `let` line below, which is the first statement of
 * this body and therefore the first line-table row of this function after its prologue.
 *
 * @param a  the left addend
 * @param b  the right addend
 * @return   the sum
 */
fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    let s = a + b
    s
}

/**
 * main — the CALLER frame of the proof, and the program entry point.
 *
 * @return the process exit status, which is the sum so a wrong result is observable from the
 *         shell without a debugger at all
 */
fn main() -> i32 {
    let r = add(2, 3)
    r
}
